WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.799
For four seasons on the History series Vikings,
Travis Fimmel starred as Ragnar Lothbrok — father,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.200
fearless warrior-king of Denmark, and the
bane of England and France.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.480
In exploits drawn from the Dark Age historical
record, Lothbrok sacked and burned his way

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.310
across the North Sea until the end of Vikings
season 4, when he was captured by Kings Ecbert

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:22.900
and Aelle, who were intent on punishing him
at last for the death of Aelle's brother Aethelwulf

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.240
in the first season — and punish him they
did.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:30.060
It was a pretty metal way to go out, too:
Lothbrok was thrown into a cage, permitted

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:34.760
to give a long, full-throated speech about
his inevitable ascent to Valhalla, and then,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.539
in an astonishingly cruel move, he was chucked
into a pit of venomous snakes for all present

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.440
to witness.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.840
It's not often that a television series will
kill off its very popular central character,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.629
so the departure from the norm — and the
sheer brutality of Ragnar's death — came

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:50.520
as a shock to most fans of the show.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.460
Why did it happen?

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.500
And was Fimmel as blindsided by the development
as Vikings' audience was?

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:00.559
Unorthodox as it may have been for Vikings
to do away with Lothbrok, history — actual

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.060
history, not the History network — has to
muscle in and take the credit for this one.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.060
Vikings is specifically a historical drama,
with emphasis on the latter half of that equation.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.680
The series draws its plotlines from the 13th
century medieval sagas of Lothbrok, a myth

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.080
of a man who was purported to have lived and
died several centuries before.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.100
According to those traditional legends, around
866 CE, Lothbrok did indeed meet his end at

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.960
the hands of King Aelle and his cruel and
creative snake pit.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.540
As for Aelle, he died the following year in
battle with the loathed Viking armies — although

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:35.270
probably not by the extremely brutal, also
quite mythical method of the "blood eagle"

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.180
depicted in the show.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.020
Lothbrok's exit, then, wasn't so much scripted
as it was written into the historical record.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.740
Fimmel's departure had always been intended,
because the program wanted to reflect the

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.380
generational changes of Dark Ages royalty,
so his character's demise came as no shock

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.000
to the actor.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.500
In fact, he actually got to remain on the
series for much longer than originally intended;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.760
initially, the idea was to have him die in
the first season as a more direct consequence

00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.760
of killing Aethelwulf.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.500
It's fortunate for us that the show's creatives
decided to give Lothbrok a stay of execution.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.280
If he had died in season one of Vikings, we
wouldn't have seen him fake his death as a

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:12.670
Trojan Horse plot in France and pop out of
a coffin, or been treated to the sight of

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.980
the mighty warrior using an unlucky priest
for archery practice.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.900
Really, it's a win for everybody that Ragnar
got to stick around for so long before his

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.780
glorious death; the only loser here is historical
accuracy, and it's not like History — the

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.740
network, we mean — ever much cared about
that.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.980
All this is to say, don't feel too bad for
Fimmel; he may have lost a pretty sweet gig,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.720
but he saw it coming, and has kept plenty
busy since his exit from Vikings.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.060
And it looks like he's going to stay that
way for the foreseeable future.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.040
In 2019 alone, he appeared in three feature
films: the crime drama Finding Steve McQueen

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.541
opposite Forest Whitaker, the bounty hunting
drama Dreamland opposite Margot Robbie, and

00:02:48.541 --> 00:02:51.040
the Australian military actioner Danger Close.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.550
The star has no fewer than three more features
in various stages of production, as well.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.120
He'll appear in the comedy El Tonto, which
has wrapped shooting; he's currently hard

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.349
at work filming the amazingly-titled Die in
a Gunfight for director Collin Schiffli, and

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:07.739
he's slated to star in the movie Tau Ceti
Four for action veteran John McTiernan, the

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.080
director of Predator and Die Hard.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.760
On top of that, he has starring gigs lined
up in two TV series: producer Ridley Scott's

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.730
sci-fi drama Raised by Wolves, which will
premiere on the upcoming HBO Max streaming

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.380
service, and a scripted anthology focusing
on legendary lawman Wyatt Earp, whom he will

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.740
portray for a familiar network - the History
channel.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.880
All in all, not a bad slate of projects for
a guy who was plucked from the obscurity of

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.160
an Australian pool hall to become one of the
hottest models Calvin Klein has ever employed.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.540
As for Vikings, the show has gone on without
Fimmel - but that, too, will soon come to

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:38.040
an end.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.980
History has announced that the series' sixth
season, which begins airing in December 2019,

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.000
will be its last.

